      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The challenge of keeping Emergency Department (ED) attendances down continues and timely access to general practice (GP) is often portrayed as a potential solution."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "One London general practice (registered population = 4900)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "QUESTION",
          "content": "Does seeing a GP before attending the ED affect the outcome of a patient's ED care?"
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Routine clinical data were extracted using SystmOne primary care computer system for all registered patients with an ED attendance between 1 October 2014 and 31 September 2015. The scanned discharge summaries from the ED and GP notes were reviewed and outcome measures extracted."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "227 patients (121 female; 104 male) attended the ED. The most common presentation was abdominal pain (n = 11). 25% of patients had seen (n = 50), or contacted by phone (n = 6), a GP about the same presenting complaint before attending the ED. Of those, 73% (n = 41/56) were referred to the ED and 49% (n = 20/41) were admitted versus 33% (n = 60/184) who self-presented (statistically significant, p = 0.05). An additional 32% of those who saw the GP first (n = 13/41) received specialist ED treatment."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION/CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Only 25% of patients see their GP prior to attending the ED. The majority of patients who were referred by their GP required admission or specialised ED treatment. It remains unclear why the majority of patients did not choose to contact their GP prior to attending the ED, despite urgent appointments being offered; research into patients' health beliefs in this group is required for greater understanding."
        }
      ]},
